### 2. ICML Track: Governance Layering & Lakehouse Profiling

#### Implementation Action Plan
*   **Step-by-Step Execution**: Set up an active [DSPy compiler framework](https://miptgirl.medium.com/programming-not-prompting-a-hands-on-guide-to-dspy-04ea2d966e6d) that reads live Apache Iceberg transactional schemas. Implement the **Governance-Aware Semantic Compiler (GASC)** to parse row and column access control tokens into sparse optimization arrays. This allows the system to filter out safe prompt fields dynamically rather than passing unpruned text strings into the context window.
*   **Evaluation Baseline Matrix**: Benchmark GASC prompt performance directly against standard [DSPy configurations](https://blog.kevinhu.me/2025/06/22/Agentic-Programming/) that lack metadata pruning routines.
*   **Core Measurement Metric**: Measure strict token footprint consumption reduction, end-to-end database query latency execution improvements, and compliance policy safety enforcement verification.

#### Dataset Validation Metrics
*   **Volumetric Capacity**: minimum 1,500 distinct analytical schema queries.
*   **Complexity Envelope**: Lakehouse catalog test cases must feature at least 100 heavily clustered relational database tables, 20 nested business metric mappings, and complex Column-Level Security constraints.
*   **Sourcing Method**: Utilize the open-source **Spider 2.0 Text-to-SQL Benchmark**, enriched with mock governance metadata blocks modeled after typical multi-tenant operational environments (e.g., [Microsoft Fabric layouts](https://microsoft.com)).

